{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And Rachel stole the terafim.</b> The opinion of the Ramban concerning the terafim is that they were instruments for receiving the hours (astrological moments), and they would divine by them to know future events. And the word is derived from the root of נרפים (slack, weak), and they called them terafim to allude concerning them that their words are like weak prophecy, coming in abundance and proving false at distant intervals. And they would speak by way of the science of the hours, and this is what is written (Zechariah 10:2): ״כי התרפים דברו און״ — 'for the terafim have spoken vanity.' And why did she steal them? So that they would not reveal to her father if he inquired of them, that Jacob was fleeing. And what Laban said to Jacob — למה גנבת את אלהי — 'why have you stolen my gods' — is a sign and testimony that the terafim were his gods." "<b>And behold</b> Laban was a diviner and a soothsayer, in the manner of what is written: נחשתי — 'I divined'; and his land, Haran, is the land of the East (קדם), full of divinations, as it is written (Isaiah 2:6): ״כי מלאו מקדם״ — 'because they are filled from the east.' Also Balaam son of Beor the diviner was from there, as it is written (Numbers 23:7): ״מן ארם ינחני בלק מלך מואב מהררי קדם״ — 'from Aram did Balak king of Moab bring me, from the mountains of the east.'" "<b>And R. Abraham</b> of blessed memory, my kinsman, wrote that the terafim were in the form of a human being, and it is a form made to receive the power of the supernal beings, and I cannot explain it. And the proof of this is the terafim into which Michal placed the image, so that the guardians of the house thought it was David. And what Scripture says (Hosea 3:4): ״ואין אפוד ותרפים״ — 'and without ephod or terafim' — means they would neither serve HaShem nor idolatry; end of quote." "<b>And our master</b> Hananel wrote that the reason she stole them was so that he might reconsider and say: a stolen god has no substance — as Joash said (Judges 6:31): ״אם אלהים הוא ירב לו כי נתץ את מזבחו״ — 'if he is a god, let him plead for himself, because someone has torn down his altar.' And as Scripture says (Ezekiel 28:9): ״האמור תאמר אלהים אני לפני הורגך ואתה אדם ולא אל ביד מחללך״ — 'will you still say, before the one who kills you, \"I am a god\"? But you are a man and not a god, in the hand of the one who wounds you.'" "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> why did she steal them? So that they would not tell that Jacob was fleeing. But is it not written (Psalms 115): ״לא יהגו בגרונם״ — 'they do not speak with their throat'? They said: no, for it is written (Zechariah 10:2): ״כי התרפים דברו און״ — 'for the terafim have spoken vanity.' How is it done? They bring a firstborn human being, slaughter him, salt him with salt and spices, and write on a plate of gold the name of an impure spirit, and place the plate with sorcery under his tongue, and set him in a wall, and light lamps before him and bow down to him, and he speaks with them in a whisper — and this is what is written: ״כי התרפים דברו און״. And the reason she stole them was to uproot idol worship from her father's house. And so the Holy One, blessed be He, promised to remove idolatry from the world, as it is written (Isaiah 2): ״והאלילים כליל יחלף״ — 'and the idols shall utterly pass away.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:17:04.396956202Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}